{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"file-streams","title":"File I/O with fstream","group":"Errors, Streams & Utilities","order":66,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>&lt;fstream&gt;</code> gives you <code>std::ofstream</code> (write), <code>std::ifstream</code> (read) and <code>std::fstream</code> (both). They work exactly like <code>cin</code>/<code>cout</code> and close themselves (RAII).</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file-pencil\"></i>Write, then read back</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;cstdio&gt;\n#include &lt;fstream&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    {\n        std::ofstream out(\"scores.txt\");                 // truncates / creates\n        if (!out) { std::cerr &lt;&lt; \"cannot open\\n\"; return 1; }\n        out &lt;&lt; \"ada 91\\n\" &lt;&lt; \"bob 78\\n\" &lt;&lt; \"cy 85\\n\";\n    }                                                     // closed (and flushed) here\n\n    {\n        std::ofstream app(\"scores.txt\", std::ios::app);  // append mode\n        app &lt;&lt; \"dee 99\\n\";\n    }\n\n    std::ifstream in(\"scores.txt\");\n    std::string name;\n    int score, total = 0, count = 0;\n    while (in &gt;&gt; name &gt;&gt; score) {                         // token-by-token\n        total += score;\n        ++count;\n    }\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; count &lt;&lt; \" students, average \" &lt;&lt; total / count &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    in.clear();                                           // reset EOF flag…\n    in.seekg(0);                                          // …and rewind\n    std::string line;\n    std::getline(in, line);                               // line-by-line\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \"first line: \" &lt;&lt; line &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    in.close();\n    std::remove(\"scores.txt\");\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>4 students, average 88\nfirst line: ada 91</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mode flag</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>std::ios::in</code> / <code>std::ios::out</code></td><td>Read / write (defaults for <code>ifstream</code> / <code>ofstream</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::ios::app</code></td><td>Every write goes to the end</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::ios::trunc</code></td><td>Discard existing contents (default for <code>ofstream</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::ios::binary</code></td><td>No newline translation (matters on Windows)</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::ios::ate</code></td><td>Start at the end, but may seek anywhere</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-traffic-lights\"></i>Stream state</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>True when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>if (stream)</code> / <code>!stream.fail()</code></td><td>Last operation succeeded</td></tr><tr><td><code>stream.eof()</code></td><td>Tried to read past the end</td></tr><tr><td><code>stream.fail()</code></td><td>Format error or EOF (e.g. reading letters into an <code>int</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>stream.bad()</code></td><td>Unrecoverable I/O error</td></tr><tr><td><code>stream.clear()</code></td><td>Resets the flags so you can continue</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>while (!in.eof()) { in &gt;&gt; x; … }</code> processes the last value twice (or garbage) because <code>eof()</code> only becomes true <em>after</em> a read fails. Loop on the read itself: <code>while (in &gt;&gt; x)</code> / <code>while (std::getline(in, line))</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-binary\"></i>Binary files &amp; whole-file reads</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;cstdio&gt;\n#include &lt;fstream&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;sstream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; data{1, 2, 3, 1000000};\n    {\n        std::ofstream out(\"data.bin\", std::ios::binary);\n        out.write(reinterpret_cast&lt;const char*&gt;(data.data()), data.size() * sizeof(int));\n    }\n    std::ifstream in(\"data.bin\", std::ios::binary);\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; back(4);\n    in.read(reinterpret_cast&lt;char*&gt;(back.data()), back.size() * sizeof(int));\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; in.gcount() &lt;&lt; \" bytes, last = \" &lt;&lt; back[3] &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::ofstream(\"note.txt\") &lt;&lt; \"line one\\nline two\\n\";\n    std::ifstream t(\"note.txt\");\n    std::stringstream buffer;\n    buffer &lt;&lt; t.rdbuf();                                  // slurp the whole file\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; buffer.str().size() &lt;&lt; \" chars\\n\";\n    std::remove(\"data.bin\");\n    std::remove(\"note.txt\");\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>16 bytes, last = 1000000\n18 chars</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">std::filesystem</div><p>For paths, directories, file sizes and existence checks, use <code>&lt;filesystem&gt;</code> (C++17): <code>std::filesystem::exists(p)</code>, <code>file_size(p)</code>, <code>directory_iterator</code>, <code>create_directories</code>.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Do you need to call close() on an fstream?</summary><div><p>Usually not: the destructor flushes and closes the file when the stream goes out of scope (RAII). Call <code>close()</code> explicitly when you want to reuse the stream object, check for errors on close, or make sure data is on disk before doing something else with the file.</p></div></details></section>"}